Per the write up: the client passes destination, activity, transport, accommodation, season, and tag; the procedure inserts each child record, links them through the itinerary table, fires the duration and cost triggers, and returns the new itinerary_id, in one round trip, validated and applied atomically. It replaces five separate inserts plus application layer checks. Sketch in T-SQL:

```sql
CREATE PROCEDURE sp_add_full_itinerary_single @dest INT, ... AS
BEGIN
  SET XACT_ABORT ON;  -- any runtime error aborts and rolls back
  BEGIN TRY
    BEGIN TRANSACTION;
      INSERT INTO activity(...) VALUES (...);   SET @act = SCOPE_IDENTITY();
      -- transport, accommodation, season, tag inserts likewise
      INSERT INTO itinerary(...) VALUES (...); SET @itin = SCOPE_IDENTITY();
      INSERT INTO itinerary_activity VALUES (@itin, @act);
    COMMIT;
    SELECT @itin AS itinerary_id;
  END TRY
  BEGIN CATCH
    IF @@TRANCOUNT > 0 ROLLBACK;
    THROW;
  END CATCH
END;
```

MySQL equivalent: START TRANSACTION, DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION BEGIN ROLLBACK; RESIGNAL; END, and LAST_INSERT_ID() for generated keys. Without the explicit transaction, each INSERT autocommits and a failure halfway leaves orphan child rows. The write up does not name the engine; use the dialect you wrote.
